<description>
Extract the top-left or top-right 2x2 corner crop selected by the top edge clue.

Input structure
- The input is a square color grid whose answer is one of the two 2x2 crops
  touching the top edge and a grid corner.
- A repeated color on the top row, when present, acts as the primary side cue.
- Otherwise, the two top corner colors have different whole-grid frequencies.

Rule
- If a top-row color repeats, find the repeated top-row occurrence nearest to
  a left or right edge and take the 2x2 top-corner crop on that side.
- If the top row has no repeated color, compare the whole-grid frequencies of
  the two top-corner colors and take the side whose corner color is rarer.

Key insight
- The task is not asking for an arbitrary 2x2 search: the decisive information
  is on the top edge, and it selects which top corner crop to keep.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "symmetry/pattern",
  "src": "top edge of the square color grid, using repeated top-row colors or the two top corner colors",
  "dst": "the 2x2 crop touching the top edge and the selected grid corner",
  "transform": "if a top-row color repeats, crop the top corner nearest to the closest repeated occurrence; otherwise crop the side whose top-corner color is rarer in the full grid",
  "grid_shape": "2x2"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: square NxN, N in 3-8
  output: 2x2 crop from top-left or top-right corner of grid
  colors: palette 0-9, grid uses 6-10 distinct colors
  mechanism: one of two mutually exclusive cues selects the side
  cue_A_repetition:
    condition: exactly 1 color appears exactly 2 times in top row (all others distinct)
    rule: |
      for each repeated occurrence, compute left_dist and right_dist to edges;
      skip if equal; otherwise emit (min_dist, side) candidate.
      Pick the candidate with smallest dist; must be unique. Crop that side.
  cue_B_rarer_corner:
    condition: all top-row colors are distinct (no repeats)
    rule: |
      count whole-grid frequency of top-left vs top-right corner colors;
      they must differ. Crop the side whose corner color is rarer.
  body: remaining cells filled freely with colors 0-9; no structural constraints

generation_strategy:
  approach: direct construction (task is simple enough)
  steps:
    - pick N uniformly from 3-8; choose mechanism A or B
    - choose target side (left or right)
    - mechanism A (repetition):
        - build top row with N-1 distinct colors, then duplicate one non-corner color
          at two positions such that the solver's nearest-candidate logic yields target side
        - fill rest of grid randomly with colors 0-9
    - mechanism B (rarer corner):
        - build top row with N fully distinct colors
        - fill rest of grid randomly, then adjust counts so that the target-side
          corner color has strictly fewer whole-grid occurrences than the other corner color
    - output = grid[:2, :2] if left else grid[:2, -2:]
```
</generation_spec>
</reference_puzzle>

